pages.searchText:
the defined-or operator use the so-called _defined-or_ operator `//` to get a fallback value if a variable is not yet set. my $a = 'alpha';
say $a // 'gamma';

my $b;
say $b // 'delta'; this program prints: alpha
delta the value of `$a` is set in the first line, so in the expression `$a // 'gamma'`, the current value of `$a` is used. in contrast, the `$b` variable was not initialised, so `$b // 'delta'` returns the right-hand-side operand, and the program prints `delta`. //= the combination of `//` and `=` gives the `//=` operator that assigns a value if the variable is not defined. my $x;
$x //= 42;
say $x; # 42

