arrays an array is an ordered container whose elements are themselves scalar containers. my @array = 100, 200, 300;
my @letters = 'a', 'b', 'c'; the `@` sigil indicates that such a variable behaves as a `positional` object, so you can subscript it to access individual elements: say @array[1];   # 200
say @letters[2]; # c because every element is a scalar container, you can assign a new value to it, exactly as you would with a standalone scalar variable: my @array = 100, 200, 300;
@array[0] = 10;
say @array; # [10 200 300] you can also assign a value of a different type, for example: my @array = 100, 200, 300;
@array[0] = 'ten';
say @array; # [ten 200 300] this is what makes arrays _mutable_: each slot is a container that can receive a new value. as you will see in the next topic, [lists](/advanced/ordered-containers/lists) behave differently.

