the substitution operator the substitution operator is `s///`. it looks like a match with a second slot: the pattern goes between the first two slashes and the replacement text between the last two: my $s = 'hello world';
$s ~~ s/world/raku/;
say $s; # hello raku substitution changes the variable **in place**, so `$s` itself is updated. the text in the replacement part is literal — you do not put it in quotes. like matching, substitution touches only the **first** occurrence by default. add the `:g` adverb to replace every occurrence: my $s = 'a-b-c';
$s ~~ s:g/'-'/_/;
say $s; # a_b_c if you want to keep the original and produce a changed **copy** instead of modifying in place, use the **uppercase** `s///` operator: it performs the substitution and returns the changed string, leaving the original untouched. because it does not update a variable in place, point it at one with `given` rather than `~~`: my $orig = 'color';
say s:g/o/0/ given $orig; # c0l0r
say $orig;                # color (writing `$orig ~~ s///` warns you to use `given` instead — with `s///` there is nothing to modify in place.) the same non-destructive behaviour is also available as a method, `.subst`, which returns a new string: my $orig = 'color';
say $orig.subst('o', '0', :g); # c0l0r
say $orig;                     # color what does the following program print? my $s = 'one two two';
