    proto token number {*}
    token number:sym<int>   { \d+ }
    token number:sym<float> { \d+ '.' \d+ }
}

say number.parse('42').defined;   # true
say number.parse('3.14').defined; # true the `proto token number {*}` line says “a `number` is one of the variants below”. each variant carries a `:sym<…>` label that names it. when the grammar needs a `<number>`, it tries the variants and, by longest-token matching, picks the one that fits — `int` for `42`, `float` for `3.14`. proto tokens read better than a long chain of `|` alternatives, and the `:sym<…>` labels give each case a name you can act on later when you attach meaning to the parse. they are the idiomatic way to express “one of these kinds” in a grammar. what does the following program print? grammar base {
    token top  { <word> }
    token word { 'cat' }
