pages.searchText:
closing a channel when a producer has no more values to send, it calls `.close` on the channel. closing signals to the consumer that the stream has ended. the most convenient way to consume a closed channel is `.list`, which gives you every remaining value and finishes once the channel is closed: my $c = channel.new;
$c.send(1);
$c.send(2);
$c.send(3);
$c.close;

say $c.list; # (1 2 3) because `.list` waits for the channel to be closed, it is perfect for “collect everything the producer sends”. you can treat the result like any other list — sum it, count it, and so on: my $c = channel.new;
$c.send($_) for 1..5;
$c.close;

say [+] $c.list; # 15 closing is how a consumer knows when to stop. without it, `.list` (or a loop of `.receive`) would wait forever for a value that never comes. always close a channel when the sending is done. what does the following program print? my $c = channel.new;
$c.send(1);
$c.send(2);
$c.close;
say $c.list.elems; 0 1 2 it waits forever

