say @b; # [1 2 3] both taps received all three values. the supply did not “use up” its values on the first tap; it simply ran again for the second. this is the behaviour you want when a supply represents a fixed source — a list, a file, a query — that every subscriber should see in full. on-demand is the default for supplies built from existing data, because each consumer naturally expects the complete sequence. the other kind, a _live_ supply, behaves quite differently, as the next topic shows. for an **on-demand** supply such as `supply.from-list`, what does a second tap receive? nothing — the first tap used up the values only the values emitted after it tapped the whole sequence, from the start an error

