awaiting a promise `await` takes a promise, waits for it to complete, and returns its result: my $p = start { 7 * 6 };
say await $p; # 42 the program pauses at `await` only as long as needed, then continues with the value the promise produced. given several promises, `await` waits for **all** of them and returns their results in the same order as the promises in the list: my @jobs = (start { 5 }), (start { 10 }), (start { 15 });
say await @jobs;     # (5 10 15)
say [+] await @jobs; # 30 the jobs run concurrently, but `await @jobs` does not return until every one is done. each result then sits at the same position as its promise in `@jobs` — no matter which job happened to finish first. if an awaited promise was **broken** — its block threw an exception — `await` rethrows that exception at the point of the `await`. this means errors in background work surface where you wait for it, so you can handle them with the usual `try` / `catch` from the part on exceptions: my $p = start { die 'no such file' };

my $result = try await $p;
say $result // "the job failed: $!"; this prints: the job failed: no such file the `die` happens on a background thread, but the exception travels with the promise and pops out at `await`. the `try` catches it there, leaving `$result` undefined and the error in `$!` — so a failure in concurrent work is handled exactly like any other exception. awaiting is therefore not just about getting a value; it is the moment where concurrent work rejoins the main flow, results and errors alike. what does the following program print? my $p = start { 8 * 8 };
say await $p; promise 8 64 true  what does the following program do? my $p = start { sleep 2; 42 };
say 'waiting';
say await $p; prints `waiting` at once, then prints `42` about two seconds later pauses for two seconds, then prints `waiting` and `42` together prints `waiting` and `42` at once — the sleep happens in the background prints `waiting`, then `promise` — you cannot await a sleeping promise

